.TH WATCH 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
watch \- run a command repeatedly and show its output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B watch
.RB [ \-n
.IR sec ]
.RB [ \-dtx ]
.I prog
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B watch
runs
.I prog
over and over.
It clears the screen before each run, so that the output stays in one
place and its changes stand out.
It runs until it is interrupted, as with Ctrl+C.
This
.B watch
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SS Cycle
Each cycle writes the clear-screen sequence, then the header, then runs
the command and waits for it to end, then waits for the period.
The period is 2 seconds unless
.B \-n
says otherwise.
It starts when the command ends, so a cycle takes the period plus the
run time of the command.
The exit status of the command is ignored.
.PP
The clear-screen sequence is the 6 bytes that
.BR clear (1)
writes, which move the cursor to the top left corner and erase from
there to the end of the screen.
They are written even when standard output is not a console, and then
they appear in the output.
.PP
The command shares the standard input, output, and error of
.BR watch .
.B watch
reads no input and does not capture the output, so output taller than
the window scrolls the header off the top.
.SS Header
Unless
.B \-t
is given, the header is a line of text and an empty line after it.
The text is
.B "Every 2.0s:"
and the command line, where
.B 2.0
stands for the period in seconds, shown with one decimal place.
The command line is the words of
.I prog
and
.I arg
joined by single spaces, whether or not
.B \-x
is given, so quoting is not shown.
The local date and time, as
.BR "YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS" ,
is written over the right end of the line, ending in the second-to-last
column.
That cuts the line short, so a long command line is truncated.
With a 60-column window the header of
.B watch ls \-l
looks like this:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Every 2.0s: ls \-l                       2026\-10\-19 21:34:22
.EE
.RE
.PP
The time is taken when the header is written, before the command runs.
When the width is 20 columns or less there is no time, and the line is
not cut.
The width is that of the console window, as described under
.BR COLUMNS ,
and is measured again in every cycle, so that resizing the window takes
effect at the next one.
.SS Command
Without
.BR \-x ,
the words of
.I prog
and
.I arg
are joined by single spaces into one command line, which
.B sh \-c
runs, so shell syntax works: pipes, redirections, wildcards, quoting,
and
.BR && .
The shell is the kit's
.BR sh (1),
whatever
.B SHELL
says, unless
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
replaces that applet.
It starts afresh in every cycle: it has none of the aliases,
functions, or unexported variables of the shell that started
.BR watch ,
and expands wildcards anew.
Quote the command so that the invoking shell passes it on whole.
The joining loses the quoting of the words, so an argument with a space
in it becomes several words.
Use
.B \-x
to keep it whole.
.PP
With
.BR \-x ,
no shell runs.
.I prog
is started directly, with each
.I arg
as one argument, so nothing is interpreted or expanded, and a shell
built-in or function cannot be run.
.I prog
is found as described under
.B Running commands
in
.BR busybox (1):
an applet of that name runs in preference to a program in
.BR PATH ,
and any other name without a directory part is looked up in
.BR PATH .
An applet that busybox marks
.BR NOFORK ,
such as
.B echo
or
.BR printf ,
runs inside the
.B watch
process itself, and any other applet in another busybox process; see
.B \-\-list\-full
in
.BR busybox (1).
If
.I prog
cannot be started, nothing is reported and
.B watch
tries again in the next cycle.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognized only before
.IR prog .
The first argument that is not an option begins
.IR prog ,
and everything after it belongs to the command, even arguments that
begin with
.BR \- .
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-tx ,
and the argument of
.B \-n
may be attached, as in
.BR \-n5 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
At least one argument must follow the options.
.TP
.B \-d
Accepted for compatibility and ignored.
Differences between runs are not highlighted.
.TP
.BI "\-n " sec
Wait
.I sec
seconds between runs.
The default is 2.
.I sec
is a decimal number of seconds that may have a fractional part and a
unit suffix of
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
.BR h ,
or
.BR d ,
as in
.B 0.5
or
.BR 1.5m .
A period of 0 runs the command again at once.
If
.B \-n
is given more than once, the last one is used.
The syntax and error messages are those of one
.BR sleep (1)
argument, except that
.B inf
is an invalid number.
.TP
.B \-t
Do not write the header, neither the line of text nor the empty line
after it.
.TP
.B \-x
Run
.I prog
directly instead of with
.BR "sh \-c" ;
see
.BR Command .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width in columns that the header is fitted to.
A value that does not begin with a number from 2 to 29999 means 80.
When
.B COLUMNS
is unset, the width is that of the console window of standard output, or
else of standard error, and 80 if neither is a console.
.TP
.B PATH
With
.BR \-x ,
where
.I prog
is looked up.
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets to replace with a program in
.BR PATH ,
both for
.I prog
with
.B \-x
and for
.B sh
without it; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone of the time in the header.
When it is unset, the time zone is the Windows setting.
.TP
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ", " BB_TERMINAL_MODE ", " CONEMUPID
Decide whether the console interprets the clear-screen sequence or
busybox emulates it, as for
.BR clear (1).
They are read only when standard output is a console.
.PP
Console output also depends on
.BR LC_ALL ,
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
and
.BR LANG .
These and the variables that busybox reads when it starts, such as
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.B watch
sets no variables itself, but busybox sets
.B BB_GLOBBING
to
.B 0
when it starts if it is unset, and the command inherits that.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The first argument was
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
An option is invalid, there is no
.IR prog ,
or
.I sec
is invalid.
A message beginning with
.BR watch: ,
the usage message, or both are written to standard error.
.TP
.B 141
With
.BR \-x ,
a
.B NOFORK
applet wrote to standard output, a pipe whose reader had exited.
.PP
Otherwise
.B watch
runs until it is interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch a directory listing, refreshed every second:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-n 1 ls \-l build
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the end of a log without a header.
The
.B \-n
after the command name belongs to
.BR tail :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-t tail \-n 5 build.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Count object files with a pipeline, twice a second.
The quotes pass the command to
.B watch
as one argument:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-n 0.5 \(aqls *.o | wc \-l\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a program without a shell, so that an argument with a space stays
whole:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-x ls \-l "my dir"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a command every minute and a half:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ watch \-n 1.5m \(aqls dist | wc \-l\(aq
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the procps
.BR watch :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-t ,
and
.B \-x
are accepted, and
.B \-d
does nothing.
There are no long options except
.BR \-\-help .
.IP \(bu 2
The output of the command is not captured, so it is not cut to the size
of the window.
.PP
Only the visible part of the console window is cleared, and text that has
scrolled above it stays in the scrollback; see
.BR clear (1).
.PP
The period is rounded down to whole milliseconds and passed to the
Windows
.B Sleep
function, so a period shorter than a millisecond does not wait, and a
period of 2^32 milliseconds, about 49.7 days, or more wraps around as
described in
.BR sleep (1).
.PP
.B watch
does not check for errors writing its own output, so when standard
output is a pipe whose reader has exited, it keeps running.
The exception is a
.B NOFORK
applet run with
.BR \-x ,
which runs inside
.BR watch :
when it writes to such a pipe,
.B watch
exits with status 141.
Ctrl+C is sent to every process attached to the console, so it also
reaches a command that is running at that moment.
.PP
When
.B BB_GLOBBING
is unset, as when
.B watch
is started from
.BR cmd.exe ,
busybox expands the wildcards in its arguments once, as it starts, so
.B watch ls *.c
names the files present at that moment.
Setting
.B BB_GLOBBING
to
.B 0
prevents this; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR clear (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR sleep (1),
.BR timeout (1)
